---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'supported', label: 'Supported targets' },
   { id: 'default', label: 'Target selection & fallback hierarchy' },
   { id: 'virtual', label: 'Virtual screen reader' },
   { id: 'real', label: 'Real OS screen readers (VoiceOver & NVDA)' },
   { id: 'choose', label: 'Choosing the right target' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Screen readers"
   summary="<p>Automated accessibility testing requires different trade-offs at different stages of development. <strong>a11ied</strong> provides three execution targets: a zero-setup <strong>virtual screen reader</strong> for instant in-process assertions, and real automation for <abbr title='Apple&apos;s built-in screen reader for macOS'><a href='https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>VoiceOver</a></abbr> on macOS and <abbr title='NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows'><a href='https://www.nvaccess.org/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>NVDA</a></abbr> on Windows.</p><p>Pass <code>--sr &lt;target&gt;</code> to select an engine, and inspect detailed runtime metadata on every response.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="supported">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Target matrix</p>
         <h2>Supported screen reader engines</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>virtual</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Headless & In-process (Cross-platform)</strong>. A complete software screen reader
               engine that computes the <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> accessibility tree
               and announcement sequence in memory or headless Chromium. Requires zero system permissions,
               operates identically across macOS, Windows, and Linux, and executes in milliseconds inside unit tests or CI pipelines.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>voiceover</code>
            <span>
               <strong>macOS Native Assistive Technology</strong>. Automates Apple's built-in <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> through
               AppleScript and <abbr title="Open-source library for driving VoiceOver and NVDA with code"><a href="https://guidepup.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Guidepup</a></abbr>.
               Interacts with the real operating system accessibility API and can record synchronized video to <code>.mov</code> files.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>nvda</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Windows Open-Source Assistive Technology</strong>. Automates the <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr> screen
               reader on Windows through Guidepup and native platform hooks. Captures spoken speech output and supports <code>.mp4</code> screen recording.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Every response from an <code>a1 sr</code> command includes precise provenance metadata:
      </p>
      <ul>
         <li><code>target</code>: The requested engine (<code>virtual</code>, <code>voiceover</code>, or <code>nvda</code>).</li>
         <li><code>targetType</code>: <code>real</code> for native OS screen readers or <code>simulated</code> for virtual mode.</li>
         <li><code>engine</code>: For virtual sessions, reports <code>browser</code> (headless Chromium running live client JavaScript) or <code>jsdom</code> (in-memory DOM).</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="default">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Automation defaults</p>
         <h2>Target selection & fallback hierarchy</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         When you invoke <code>a1 sr start</code> without specifying an explicit <code>--sr</code> flag,
         <code>a11ied</code> intelligently selects the appropriate target based on your current operating system
         and environment readiness:
      </p>
      <ol>
         <li><strong>Platform detection</strong>: On macOS, it defaults to <code>voiceover</code>. On Windows, it defaults to <code>nvda</code>. On Linux, CI containers, and Docker environments, it defaults to <code>virtual</code>.</li>
         <li><strong>Readiness check</strong>: If native VoiceOver or NVDA is detected but <code>a1 doctor</code> reports that required system permissions or preference bundles are missing (<code>requires-setup</code>), the session automatically falls back to <code>virtual</code>.</li>
         <li><strong>Transparent notice</strong>: When a fallback occurs, <code>a11ied</code> emits a clear warning in the output stating that a virtual simulation ran rather than native assistive technology, along with the <code>a1 doctor</code> command to configure native support.</li>
      </ol>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="virtual">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Headless simulation</p>
            <h2>Virtual screen reader</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            The virtual screen reader is engineered for maximum speed and deterministic reproducibility.
            Because native screen readers take control of system focus and audio, running them in automated
            watch loops or shared CI workers is often impractical.
         </p>
         <p>
            When starting a virtual session explicitly on macOS or Windows, pass <code>--sr virtual</code>:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout</code></pre>
         <p>
            <strong>Execution modes:</strong>
         </p>
         <ul>
            <li>
               <strong>Browser engine (Default for URLs)</strong>: When passed an HTTP/HTTPS URL, the virtual
               reader spins up a headless Chromium instance. All client-side JavaScript, custom elements, CSS transitions,
               and dynamic DOM mutations (such as modal popups and live status updates) execute exactly as they do in production.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>JSDOM engine (In-memory testing)</strong>: When testing raw HTML strings or running isolated unit tests
               without a local web server, the virtual reader operates in-memory using jsdom for sub-millisecond execution.
            </li>
         </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">AI agent friendly</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Because the virtual engine requires no audio hardware and produces structured JSON logs,
            it is the primary target used by AI coding assistants via the <abbr title="Model Context Protocol: open standard for connecting AI coding assistants to external tools"><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a></abbr> server.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="real">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Operating system fidelity</p>
         <h2>Real OS screen readers (VoiceOver & NVDA)</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Real screen readers interact directly with the operating system's accessibility tree and speech synthesizer.
         When you start a session targeting <code>voiceover</code> or <code>nvda</code>, <code>a11ied</code> launches
         your system browser, brings its window to the foreground, and attaches Guidepup automation:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout
a1 sr next</code></pre>
      <p>
         <strong>Window management:</strong> If you switch windows while inspecting tests, run <code>a1 sr focus</code>
         to restore the test browser to the foreground. You can also target specific processes using <code>--app</code>,
         <code>--bundle-id</code>, <code>--process</code>, or <code>--window-title</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <strong>One-time machine setup:</strong> Before running native screen readers for the first time,
         run <code>a1 setup</code> to install necessary preference bundles and verify OS permissions:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 setup</code></pre>
      <p>
         On macOS, this enables AppleScript control within VoiceOver settings and installs the Guidepup testing preferences bundle.
         To record video demonstrations of tests in action, grant Screen Recording permissions to your terminal or host app in macOS System Settings.
         See the <a href="/guides/recording">recording guide</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="choose">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Architecture guide</p>
         <h2>Choosing the right target</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Use virtual when:</strong>
            <span>
               You are writing unit or component tests with <code>a11ied/vitest</code>; running pull request validation in Linux CI/CD pipelines;
               running fast TDD watch loops during development; or connecting AI coding agents via MCP.
               Virtual testing is instantaneous, headless, and identical across developer machines.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Use VoiceOver or NVDA when:</strong>
            <span>
               You need to verify platform-specific accessibility quirks; test keyboard rotor or elements list navigation;
               confirm cursor placement across complex OS dialog transitions; create video evidence for QA or compliance audits;
               or investigate bug reports originating from real assistive technology users.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>
</DocShell>
